the callee-saved registers, so setjmp can cross a convention boundary.

M1 ASSEMBLES FROM A FIXED VOCABULARY and had no load or store for x19-x28 at
all -- not one form, for any base register. That was fine while setjmp only had
to serve micro-c, whose convention uses x13, x17, x18 and lr and nothing else.

IT STOPPED BEING FINE AT tcc's -run. The program tcc compiles into memory is
TCC-generated, so it follows AAPCS64 and freely uses x19-x28, the frame pointer
and the real sp. When it calls exit, tccrun.c's rt_exit longjmps back to a
setjmp made in micro-c-compiled code, across the boundary between the two
conventions. Restoring four registers left the other twelve holding whatever
the JIT'd program had put there:

    mc-tcc -run hello.c    ->  "hello from -run", then SIGSEGV on the way out

setjmp-aarch64.c had predicted this in its own header -- "Linking this against
code from any other compiler would be wrong, and that is worth stating plainly
rather than discovering later."

WHAT IS ADDED. Stores through x1 and loads through x14, which is how setjmp and
longjmp already address the buffer, for x19 through x29 at offsets 32..112, the
real stack pointer at 120, and the four words longjmp reads back through x14.
Twenty-eight defines.

THE ENCODINGS ARE DERIVED, NOT GUESSED:

    str xN,[xM,#off]   0xF9000000 | (off/8 << 10) | (M << 5) | N
    ldr xN,[xM,#off]   0xF9400000 | (off/8 << 10) | (M << 5) | N

both little-endian. The formula was checked against eight entries ALREADY in
this table before a single new line was written, and reproduced all eight:

    str_x0,[x1,8]    computed 200400f9   in the table 200400f9
    str_x0,[x1,16]   computed 200800f9   in the table 200800f9
    ... eight of eight, zero mismatches

d8-d15 ARE NOT HERE. AAPCS64 makes the low half of those callee-saved too, and
this table has no d-register load or store either. A JIT'd program keeping a
double in d8 across a call to exit would still corrupt it. Recorded rather than
left to be found.

